\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:exp-setup}

We evaluate on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based interactive environment for elementary science tasks with sparse terminal rewards and a $-100$ death penalty. Using the methodology in \S\ref{sec:collection}, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 per type). The LLM backbone is Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct served via vLLM with episodes capped at 30 steps.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task death rate differential (internal minus deterministic) vs.\ $\Delta_{\text{full}}$. Each point represents one of 11 task types. Regression: $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$).}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

The full performance gap between deterministic and internal strategies across 245 episodes is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$ points. Excluding all episodes where either strategy results in death, the gap collapses to $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$. The resulting DAF (\S\ref{sec:daf}) is 90.41\%: approximately 90\% of the deterministic strategy's advantage is attributable to death avoidance.

This concentration is consistent across task types. The Pearson correlation between per-task death rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$; Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}). The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes versus 16.3\% for deterministic---a 2.6$\times$ ratio driving the bulk of the score difference.

The label distribution reflects this asymmetry: 86 \texttt{det\_preferred}, 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, 150 \texttt{no\_preference}. When internal reasoning does not cause death, it performs comparably to deterministic computation.

One task reveals residual non-death signal: \texttt{find-non-living-thing} ($n=40$) has zero deaths yet $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$, entirely from non-death differences. Among the 10 death-prone task types, however, death avoidance overwhelms non-death signal.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-44.89$, $-33.37$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Det. & $-9.47$ & [$-13.66$, $-5.11$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-27.75$, $-17.09$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-10.65$, $-2.43$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router & $-10.70$ & [$-15.25$, $-6.38$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 task types, 245 episodes). Bootstrap CIs. The oracle selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode; 96.2\% of its decisions match always-deterministic.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} reports episode-weighted mean scores and death rates. Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points, consistent with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$. The oracle improves only modestly to $-6.58$, with 96.2\% of its decisions matching always-deterministic---\emph{oracle degeneracy} indicating the optimal routing policy closely resembles unconditional deterministic computation. Random routing ($-22.38$) falls between the two pure strategies.

Keyword-based routing fails structurally: ScienceWorld actions use generic verbs (\texttt{pick up}, \texttt{move}, \texttt{connect}) rather than science-specific terms, causing match rates to cluster at extremes---near-total (85.8\%, degenerating to always-deterministic) or negligible ($<$5\%, degenerating to always-internal).

The trained router (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) scores $-10.70$, 1.23 points below always-deterministic, with comparable death rate (17.6\% vs.\ 16.3\%).

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We investigate whether episode-level counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic probe rather than a routing system proposal.

\paragraph{Classification head.} A linear head appended to the LLM's hidden representations degenerates to always-deterministic (0\% internal recall) across all configurations (class weighting, focal loss, undersampling, temperature scaling).

\paragraph{Implicit router.} We fine-tune Qwen3-1.7B to internalize routing decisions. Training configuration significantly affects results. With gradient accumulation GA=8 (effective batch size 32), the model achieves generation accuracy of 65.4\% with internal recall of 51.5\%---the only non-degenerate configuration. With GA=1 (effective batch size 4), logit-level accuracy reaches 74.5\% (balanced accuracy 79\%), but generation degenerates to 53.3\% accuracy with only 26.7\% internal recall---a 21.2 percentage point logit--generation gap. Effective batch size determines whether learned patterns transfer to generation.

Across four seeds (GA=8), balanced accuracy is $58.1 \pm 0.8\%$---8 points above chance. Internal recall varies from 51.5\% to 81.8\%, indicating seed-dependent boundaries with consistent weak discriminability. End-to-end (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}): the router's 97.0\% simulator invocation ratio means it routes internally only 3\% of the time---weak signal with unstable thresholds, consistent with minimal routing structure beyond death avoidance.
TEXEOF
